{"abstract":"Proving explicit lower bounds on the size of algebraic formulas is a long-standing open problem in the area of algebraic complexity theory. Recent results in the area (e.g. a lower bound against constant-depth algebraic formulas due to Limaye, Srinivasan, and Tavenas (FOCS 2021)) have indicated a way forward for attacking this question: show that we can convert a general algebraic formula to a homogeneous algebraic formula with moderate blow-up in size, and prove strong lower bounds against the latter model. Here, a homogeneous algebraic formula F for a polynomial P is a formula in which all subformulas compute homogeneous polynomials. In particular, if P is homogeneous of degree d, F does not contain subformulas that compute polynomials of degree greater than d. We investigate the feasibility of the above strategy and prove a number of positive and negative results in this direction. Lower bounds against weighted homogeneous formulas: We show the first lower bounds against homogeneous formulas of any depth in the weighted setting. Here, each variable has a given weight and the weight of a monomial is the sum of weights of the variables in it. This result builds on a lower bound of Hrubeš and Yehudayoff (Computational Complexity 2011) against homogeneous multilinear formulas. This result is strong indication that lower bounds against homogeneous formulas are within reach. Improved (quasi-)homogenization for formulas: A simple folklore argument shows that any formula F for a homogeneous polynomial of degree d can be homogenized with a size blow-up of dO(logs). We show that this can be improved superpolynomially over fields of characteristic 0 as long as d = so(1). Such a result was previously only known when d = (logs)1+o(1) (Raz (J. ACM 2013)). Further, we show how to get rid of the condition on d at the expense of getting a quasi-homogenization result: this means that subformulas can compute polynomials of degree up to poly(d). Lower bounds for non-commutative homogenization: A recent result of Dutta, Gesmundo, Ikenmeyer, Jindal and Lysikov (2022) implies that to homogenize algebraic formulas of any depth, it suffices to homogenize non-commutative algebraic formulas of depth just 3. We are able to show strong lower bounds for such homogenization, suggesting barriers for this approach. No Girard-Newton identities for positive characteristic: In characteristic 0, it is known how to homogenize constant-depth algebraic formulas with a size blow-up of exp(O(√d)) using the Girard-Newton identities. Finding analogues of these identities in positive characteristic would allow us, paradoxically, to show lower bounds for constant-depth formulas over such fields. We rule out a strong generalization of Girard-Newton identities in the setting of positive characteristic, suggesting that a different approach is required.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Fournier, Hervé","Limaye, Nutan","Srinivasan, Srikanth","Tavenas, Sébastien"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-800798"],"event":{"end":"2024-06-28","place":"Vancouver, ca","start":"2024-06-24","title":"56th Annual ACM Symposium on Theory of Computing, STOC 2024","year":"2024"},"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["France"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Sorbonne University","Université Savoie Mont Blanc"],"keyword":["Algebraic formulas"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on the power of homogeneous algebraic formulas","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3618260.3649760","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-800798","identifier":[{"id":"85196675825","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-e6055b63-4199-4dd1-942e-4a5e9f39a280"],"keyword":["Algebraic Formulas","Formula Homogenization"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D2C70DAFEF152D2D48F60C288293E40A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/431873603/On_the_Power_of_Homogeneous_Algebraic_Formula.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3618260.3649760","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"fr","key":"sorbonne university","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Sorbonne University","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Algorithms and Complexity"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"fr","key":"université savoie mont blanc","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Université Savoie Mont Blanc","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Hervé","key":"fournierpau","last":"Fournier","name":"Fournier, Hervé","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Nutan","key":"limayepau","last":"Limaye","name":"Limaye, Nutan","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Srikanth","key":"srinivasanpau","last":"Srinivasan","locid":"371fe946-6671-4c83-95c0-ec52d2ed0efa","name":"Srinivasan, Srikanth","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Sébastien","key":"tavenaspau","last":"Tavenas","name":"Tavenas, Sébastien","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Bojan","key":"moharpedh","last":"Mohar","name":"Mohar, Bojan","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Igor","key":"shinkarpedh","last":"Shinkar","name":"Shinkar, Igor","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Ryan","key":"odonnellpedh","last":"O�Donnell","name":"O�Donnell, Ryan","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3618260.3649760","eisbn":["9798400703836"],"pages":"141-151","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"STOC 2024 - Proceedings of the 56th Annual ACM Symposium on Theory of Computing"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85196675825"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-e6055b63-4199-4dd1-942e-4a5e9f39a280"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"On the Power of Homogeneous Algebraic Formulas","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-800798","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Schur Polynomials are families of symmetric polynomialsthat have been classically studied in Combinatorics and Algebra alike.They play a central role in the study of symmetric functions, in Representationtheory Stanley (1999), in Schubert calculus Ledoux & Malham(2010) as well as in Enumerative combinatorics Gasharov (1996); Stanley(1984, 1999). In recent years, they have also shown up in variousincarnations in Computer Science, e.g, quantum computation Hallgrenet al. (2000); O'Donnell & Wright (2015) and geometric complexitytheory Ikenmeyer & Panova (2017). However, unlike some other families of symmetric polynomials like theelementary symmetric polynomials, the power symmetric polynomialsand the complete homogeneous symmetric polynomials, the computationalcomplexity of syntactically computing Schur polynomials has notbeen studied much. In particular, it is not known whether Schur polynomialscan be computed efficiently by algebraic formulas. In this work,we address this question and show that unless every polynomial with asmall algebraic branching program (ABP) has a small algebraic formula,there are Schur polynomials that cannot be computed by algebraic formulaof polynomial size. In other words, unless the algebraic complexityclass VBP is equal to the complexity class VF, there exist Schur polynomialswhich do not have polynomial size algebraic formulas. As a consequence of our proof, we also show that computing the determinantof certain generalized Vandermonde matrices is essentially ashard as computing the general symbolic determinant. To the best of our knowledge, these are one of the first hardness results of this kindfor families of polynomials, which are not multilinear. A key ingredientof our proof is the study of composition of well behaved algebraicallyindependent polynomials with a homogeneous polynomial, which mightbe of independent interest.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Chaugule, Prasad","Kumar, Mrinal","Limaye, Nutan","Mohapatra, Chandra Kanta","She, Adrian","Srinivasan, Srikanth"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-675383"],"filters":{"added":"2023-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, South","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Mohapatra, Chandra Kanta"],"country":["Canada","India"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Indian Institute of Technology, Bombay","Samuel Lunenfeld Research Institute, Mount Sinai Hospital, Toronto","Tata Institute of Fundamental Research"],"keyword":["Jacobian","Lower bound","Taylor expansion"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"schur polynomials do not have small formulas if the determinant does not","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85159936848","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-675383","identifier":[{"id":"85159936848","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-95d7fde2-ce23-415a-b3cb-93117ea314e7"],"keyword":["68W30","Algebraic independence","Formula complexity","Generalized Vandermonde determinant","Jacobian","Lower bound","Schur polynomial","Taylor expansion"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6A269C1F897BE645A38B88F1077D5541"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"in","key":"indian institute of technology bombay","loc":["India","Asia, South"],"name":"Indian Institute of Technology, Bombay","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"in","key":"tata institute of fundamental research","loc":["India","Asia, South"],"name":"Tata Institute of Fundamental Research","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"ca","key":"samuel lunenfeld research institute mount sinai hospital toronto","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Samuel Lunenfeld Research Institute, Mount Sinai Hospital, Toronto","used":1},{"affno":"5","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Prasad","key":"chaugulepau","last":"Chaugule","name":"Chaugule, Prasad","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mrinal","key":"kumarpau","last":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Mrinal","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Nutan","key":"limayepau","last":"Limaye","name":"Limaye, Nutan","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Chandra Kanta","key":"mohapatrapau","last":"Mohapatra","name":"Mohapatra, Chandra Kanta","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Adrian","key":"shepau","last":"She","name":"She, Adrian","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"first":"Srikanth","key":"srinivasanpau","last":"Srinivasan","locid":"dfdcb974-8de7-4eaa-8422-1bd9daecf2f0","name":"Srinivasan, Srikanth","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00037-023-00236-x","issn":["10163328","14208954"],"issn_org":["10163328"],"issue":"1","journal":"Computational Complexity","paperid":"3","publisher":"Birkhauser Verlag Basel","status":"p","title":"Computational Complexity","volume":"32"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85159936848"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-95d7fde2-ce23-415a-b3cb-93117ea314e7"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Schur Polynomials Do Not Have Small Formulas If the Determinant does not","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-675383","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Classical results of Brent, Kuck and Maruyama (IEEE Trans. Computers 1973) and Brent (JACM 1974) show that any algebraic formula of size s can be converted to one of depth Oplog sq with only a polynomial blow-up in size. In this paper, we consider a fine-grained version of this result depending on the degree of the polynomial computed by the algebraic formula. Given a homogeneous algebraic formula of size s computing a polynomial P of degree d, we show that P can also be computed by an (unbounded fan-in) algebraic formula of depth Oplog dq and size polypsq. Our proof shows that this result also holds in the highly restricted setting of monotone, non-commutative algebraic formulas. This improves on previous results in the regime when d is small (i.e., d “sop1q). In particular, for the setting of d “Oplog sq, along with a result of Raz (STOC 2010, JACM 2013), our result implies the same depth reduction even for inhomogeneous formulas. This is particularly interesting in light of recent algebraic formula lower bounds, which work precisely in this “low-degree” and “low-depth” setting. We also show that these results cannot be improved in the monotone setting, even for commutative formulas.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Fournier, Hervé","Limaye, Nutan","Malod, Guillaume","Srinivasan, Srikanth","Tavenas, Sébastien"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-693756"],"event":{"end":"2023-07-20","place":"Warwick, gb","start":"2023-07-17","title":"38th Computational Complexity Conference, CCC 2023","year":"2023"},"filters":{"added":"2023-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Fournier, Hervé","Limaye, Nutan","Malod, Guillaume","Srinivasan, Srikanth","Tavenas, Sébastien"],"country":["France"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Sorbonne Université","Universite de Savoie"],"keyword":["Algebraic formulas"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"towards optimal depth reductions for algebraic formulas","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.CCC.2023.28","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-693756","identifier":[{"id":"85168422243","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-672cc3c6-1bd3-4412-9942-7317717d21ed"],"keyword":["Algebraic formulas","depth-reduction"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-D1058B9B955B0EF45C61748871D27319"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.CCC.2023.28","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"fr","key":"sorbonne université","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Sorbonne Université","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"fr","key":"universite de savoie","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universite de Savoie","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hervé","key":"fournierpau","last":"Fournier","name":"Fournier, Hervé","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nutan","key":"limayepau","last":"Limaye","name":"Limaye, Nutan","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Guillaume","key":"malodpau","last":"Malod","name":"Malod, Guillaume","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Srikanth","key":"srinivasanpau","last":"Srinivasan","locid":"dfdcb974-8de7-4eaa-8422-1bd9daecf2f0","name":"Srinivasan, Srikanth","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Sébastien","key":"tavenaspau","last":"Tavenas","name":"Tavenas, Sébastien","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Amnon","key":"tashmapedh","last":"Ta-Shma","name":"Ta-Shma, Amnon","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4230/lipics.ccc.2023.28","eisbn":["9783959772822"],"issn":["18688969"],"publisher":"Dagstuhl Publishing","series":"Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics, LIPIcs","status":"p","title":"38th Computational Complexity Conference, CCC 2023"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85168422243"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-672cc3c6-1bd3-4412-9942-7317717d21ed"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Towards Optimal Depth-Reductions for Algebraic Formulas","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-693756","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"author_count":3,"contributors":["Limaye, Nutan","Srinivasan, Srikanth","Tavenas, Sébastien"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384542"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["France"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University Savoie Mont Blanc"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"set multilinear and non commutative formula lower bounds for iterated matrix multiplication","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2021/094","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-384542","identifier":[{"id":"85132770790","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-deb3dce2-1658-47aa-b026-9cac3467198f"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-5-4CE4304B7377D2FB5E5BF6C77C25D83B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2021/094","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"fr","key":"university savoie mont blanc","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University Savoie Mont Blanc","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nutan","key":"limayepau","last":"Limaye","locid":"3f05e4ce-6433-4c5a-b72c-aa89422cf431","name":"Limaye, Nutan","orcid":"0000-0002-0238-1674","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Srikanth","key":"srinivasanpau","last":"Srinivasan","name":"Srinivasan, Srikanth","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Sébastien","key":"tavenaspau","last":"Tavenas","name":"Tavenas, Sébastien","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3519935.3520044","pages":"416-425","status":"p","title":"STOC '22: 54th Annual ACM SIGACT Symposium on Theory of Computing"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85132770790"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-deb3dce2-1658-47aa-b026-9cac3467198f"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Set-multilinear and non-commutative formula lower bounds for iterated matrix multiplication.","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384542","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"A polynomial P ∈ F[x1,..., xn] is said to be symmetric if it is invariant under any permutation of its input variables. The study of symmetric polynomials is a classical topic in mathematics, specifically in algebraic combinatorics and representation theory. More recently, they have been studied in several works in computer science, especially in algebraic complexity theory. In this paper, we prove the computational hardness of one of the most basic kinds of symmetric polynomials: the monomial symmetric polynomials, which are obtained by summing all distinct permutations of a single monomial. This family of symmetric functions is a natural basis for the space of symmetric polynomials (over any field), and generalizes many well-studied families such as the elementary symmetric polynomials and the power-sum symmetric polynomials. We show that certain families of monomial symmetric polynomials are VNP-complete with respect to oracle reductions. This stands in stark contrast to the case of elementary and power symmetric polynomials, both of which have constant-depth circuits of polynomial size.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Curticapean, Radu","Limaye, Nutan","Srinivasan, Srikanth"],"cuid":["S02-081.33-023774"],"event":{"end":"2022-12-20","place":"Chennai, in","start":"2022-12-18","title":"42nd IARCS Annual Conference on Foundations of Software Technology and Theoretical Computer Science, FSTTCS 2022","year":"2022"},"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Curticapean, Radu","Limaye, Nutan","Srinivasan, Srikanth"],"keyword":["Algebraic complexity","Permanent"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on the vnp hardness of some monomial symmetric polynomials","src":["au","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.FSTTCS.2022.16","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-081.33-023774","identifier":[{"id":"85144250895","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-9873113d-4963-4ae2-a198-529d84ca4c32","itu-b93fd40f-ed99-45e3-933e-b86b20d1c3bd"],"keyword":["Sidon set","algebraic complexity","permanent","symmetric polynomial"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A78C4B7A2337340E6F7A6424554A5FD8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.FSTTCS.2022.16","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2022/139/","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Radu-Cristian","key":"curticapeanpau","last":"Curticapean","locid":"53c45a58-4c3c-424c-80a2-c39e733fd7c3","name":"Curticapean, Radu-Cristian","orcid":"0000-0001-7201-9905","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nutan","key":"limayepau","last":"Limaye","locid":"3f05e4ce-6433-4c5a-b72c-aa89422cf431","name":"Limaye, Nutan","orcid":"0000-0002-0238-1674","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Srikanth","key":"srinivasanpau","last":"Srinivasan","locid":"dfdcb974-8de7-4eaa-8422-1bd9daecf2f0","name":"Srinivasan, Srikanth","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Anuj","key":"dawarpedh","last":"Dawar","name":"Dawar, Anuj","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Venkatesan","key":"guruswamipedh","last":"Guruswami","name":"Guruswami, Venkatesan","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4230/lipics.fsttcs.2022.16","eisbn":["9783959772617"],"isbn":["9783959772617"],"issn":["18688969"],"publisher":"Dagstuhl Publishing","series":"Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics, LIPIcs","status":"p","title":"42nd IARCS Annual Conference on Foundations of Software Technology and Theoretical Computer Science, FSTTCS 2022"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85144250895"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-9873113d-4963-4ae2-a198-529d84ca4c32","itu-b93fd40f-ed99-45e3-933e-b86b20d1c3bd"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"On the VNP-Hardness of Some Monomial Symmetric Polynomials","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-081.33-023774","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Given a polynomial fpx1; : : : ; xNq, we can wonder how we can evaluate it with a minimal number of arithmetic operations. Indeed, the task of the algorithm designer is to nd evolved processes which decrease this number. In the other direction, it could be interesting to be able to prove that there is no algorithm which does the computation in less than some number of operations. But how one could prove something like that? We will present here some methods which allow us to tackle the problem in the case of constant-depth algebraic circuits.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Limaye, Nutan","Srinivasan, Srikanth","Tavenas, Sébastien"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384596"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["France"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University Savoie Mont Blanc"],"keyword":["Algorithmic Complexity","Arithmetic Operations","Constant Depth Algebraic Circuits","Lower Bound Proof Techniques","Polynomial Evaluation"],"level":"com","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"guest column lower bounds against constant depth algebraic circuits","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/92949831/column.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-384596","ids":["itu-e1f6c160-5aa3-4ad1-a6ed-f0b572755a5e"],"keyword":["Algorithmic Complexity","Arithmetic Operations","Constant-Depth Algebraic Circuits","Lower Bound Proof Techniques","Polynomial Evaluation"],"lang":"English","level":"Commissioned","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-8DE4D5B18ACE9F4E82F9A1391C159E46"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/92949831/column.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"fr","key":"university savoie mont blanc","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University Savoie Mont Blanc","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nutan","key":"limayepau","last":"Limaye","locid":"3f05e4ce-6433-4c5a-b72c-aa89422cf431","name":"Limaye, Nutan","orcid":"0000-0002-0238-1674","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Srikanth","key":"srinivasanpau","last":"Srinivasan","name":"Srinivasan, Srikanth","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Sébastien","key":"tavenaspau","last":"Tavenas","name":"Tavenas, Sébastien","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3544979.3544989","issn":["01635700","19435827"],"issn_org":["01635700"],"issue":"3","journal":"SIGACT News","status":"p","title":"SIGACT News","volume":"53"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e1f6c160-5aa3-4ad1-a6ed-f0b572755a5e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Guest Column: Lower Bounds Against Constant-Depth Algebraic Circuits.","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384596","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"We show that there is a randomized algorithm that, when given a small constant-depth Boolean circuit C made up of gates that compute constant-degree Polynomial Threshold functions or PTFs (i.e., Boolean functions that compute signs of constant-degree polynomials), counts the number of satisfying assignments to C in significantly better than brute-force time.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Balaji, Swapnam","Krishan, Vaibhav","Kush, Deepanshu","Limaye, Nutan","Srinivasan, Srikanth"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384116"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, South"],"country":["India"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Indian Institute of Technology Bombay"],"keyword":["Circuit Satisfiability","Memoization","PTF circuits"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a sat algorithm for small constant depth circuits with ptf gates","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2018/162/download","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384116","identifier":[{"id":"85122665619","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-c2eea90b-4567-4d1d-82a4-884e17ac5537"],"keyword":["Circuit Satisfiability","Memoization","PTF circuits"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-F238811A226B7033C23E4F5702A40800"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2018/162/","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/100246551/A_SAT_Algorithm_for_Small_Constant_Depth_Circuits.pdf","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2018/162/download","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"in","key":"indian institute of technology bombay","loc":["India","Asia, South"],"name":"Indian Institute of Technology Bombay","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Swapnam","key":"balajipau","last":"Balaji","name":"Balaji, Swapnam","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vaibhav","key":"krishanpau","last":"Krishan","name":"Krishan, Vaibhav","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Deepanshu","key":"kushpau","last":"Kush","name":"Kush, Deepanshu","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nutan","key":"limayepau","last":"Limaye","locid":"3f05e4ce-6433-4c5a-b72c-aa89422cf431","name":"Limaye, Nutan","orcid":"0000-0002-0238-1674","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Srikanth","key":"srinivasanpau","last":"Srinivasan","name":"Srinivasan, Srikanth","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00453-021-00915-7","issn":["01784617","14320541"],"issn_org":["01784617"],"journal":"Algorithmica","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Algorithmica","volume":"84"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-c2eea90b-4567-4d1d-82a4-884e17ac5537"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A #SAT Algorithm for Small Constant-Depth Circuits with PTF gates.","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384116","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"author_count":2,"contributors":["Chaugule, Prasad","Limaye, Nutan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384154"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, South"],"country":["India"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Indian Institute of Technology Bombay"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on the closures of monotone algebraic classes and variants of the determinant","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/93078837/main.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-384154","identifier":[{"id":"85144515939","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-c4d8747b-a1d9-47ed-9493-8a7e9a2feea8"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-DD30A40D6212E243977BAB61DE362411"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/93078837/main.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"in","key":"indian institute of technology bombay","loc":["India","Asia, South"],"name":"Indian Institute of Technology Bombay","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Prasad","key":"chaugulepau","last":"Chaugule","name":"Chaugule, Prasad","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nutan","key":"limayepau","last":"Limaye","locid":"3f05e4ce-6433-4c5a-b72c-aa89422cf431","name":"Limaye, Nutan","orcid":"0000-0002-0238-1674","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-20624-5_37","eisbn":["9783031206245"],"isbn":["9783031206238"],"pages":"610-625","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"LATIN 2022: Theoretical Informatics - 15th Latin American Symposium"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85144515939"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-c4d8747b-a1d9-47ed-9493-8a7e9a2feea8"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"On the Closures of Monotone Algebraic Classes and Variants of the Determinant","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384154","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Graph Isomorphism is the prime example of a computational problem with a wide difference between the best-known lower and upper bounds on its complexity. The gap between the known upper and lower bounds continues to be very significant for many subclasses of graphs as well. We bridge the gap for a natural and important class of graphs, namely, planar graphs, by presenting a log-space upper bound that matches the known log-space hardness. In fact, we show a stronger result that planar graph canonization is in log-space.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Datta, Samir","Limaye, Nutan","Nimbhorkar, Prajakta","Thierauf, Thomas","Wagner, Fabian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384912"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, South","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany","India"],"glorg":["Aalen University, Germany","All Global Affiliations","Chennai Mathematical Institute","Ulm University"],"keyword":["Computational complexity","log space","planar graph isomorphism"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"planar graph isomorphism is in log space","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3543686","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384912","identifier":[{"id":"85166613747","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-f73e611b-6bcc-40e5-8ede-12f8e2b754e1"],"keyword":["Computational complexity","log-space","planar graph isomorphism"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-11CD8B435FE2B7AC0E8A8663ECE3AF46"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101336712/3543686.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3543686","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3543686","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"in","key":"chennai mathematical institute","loc":["India","Asia, South"],"name":"Chennai Mathematical Institute","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"aalen university germany","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Aalen University, Germany","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"de","key":"ulm university","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Ulm University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Samir","key":"dattapau","last":"Datta","name":"Datta, Samir","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nutan","key":"limayepau","last":"Limaye","locid":"3f05e4ce-6433-4c5a-b72c-aa89422cf431","name":"Limaye, Nutan","orcid":"0000-0002-0238-1674","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Prajakta","key":"nimbhorkarpau","last":"Nimbhorkar","name":"Nimbhorkar, Prajakta","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"thieraufpau","last":"Thierauf","name":"Thierauf, Thomas","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Fabian","key":"wagnerpau","last":"Wagner","name":"Wagner, Fabian","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3543686","issn":["19423454","19423462"],"issn_org":["19423454"],"issue":"2","journal":"ACM Transactions on Computation Theory","pages":"1-33","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"ACM Transactions on Computation Theory","volume":"14"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85166613747"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f73e611b-6bcc-40e5-8ede-12f8e2b754e1"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Planar Graph Isomorphism Is in Log-Space","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384912","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Abstract We make progress on understanding a lower bound technique that was recently used by the authors to prove the first superpolynomial constant-depth circuit lower bounds against algebraic circuits. More specifically, our previous work applied the well-known partial derivative method in a new setting, that of lopsided set-multilinear polynomials. A set-multilinear polynomial P ∈ [X_1,…,X_d] (for disjoint sets of variables X_1,…,X_d) is a linear combination of monomials, each of which contains one variable from X_1,…,X_d. A lopsided space of set-multilinear polynomials is one where the sets X_1,…,X_d are allowed to have different sizes (we use the adjective \"lopsided\" to stress this feature). By choosing a suitable lopsided space of polynomials, and using a suitable version of the partial-derivative method for proving lower bounds, we were able to prove constant-depth superpolynomial set-multilinear formula lower bounds even for very low-degree polynomials (as long as d is a growing function of the number of variables N). This in turn implied lower bounds against general formulas of constant-depth. A priori, there is nothing stopping these techniques from giving us lower bounds against algebraic formulas of any depth. We investigate the extent to which this lower bound can extend to greater depths. We prove the following results. 1) We observe that our choice of the lopsided space and the kind of partial-derivative method used can be modeled as the choice of a multiset W ⊆ [-1,1] of size d. Our first result completely characterizes, for any product-depth Δ, the best lower bound we can prove for set-multilinear formulas of product-depth Δ in terms of some combinatorial properties of W, that we call the depth-Δ tree bias of W. 2) We show that the maximum depth-3 tree bias, over multisets W of size d, is Θ(d^{1/4}). This shows a stronger formula lower bound of N^{Ω(d^{1/4})} for set-multilinear formulas of product-depth 3, and also puts a non-trivial constraint on the best lower bounds we can hope to prove at this depth in this framework (a priori, we could have hoped to prove a lower bound of N^{Ω(Δ d^{1/Δ})} at product-depth Δ). 3) Finally, we show that for small Δ, our proof technique cannot hope to prove lower bounds of the form N^{Ω(d^{1/poly(Δ)})}. This seems to strongly hint that new ideas will be required to prove lower bounds for formulas of unbounded depth.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Limaye, Nutan","Srinivasan, Srikanth","Tavenas, Sébastien"],"cuid":["S02-082.65-016078"],"event":{"end":"2022-07-23","place":"Philadelphia, us","start":"2022-07-20","title":"37th Computational Complexity Conference, CCC 2022","year":"2022"},"filters":{"added":"2022-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Asia, South","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["France","India"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Indian Institute of Technology Bombay","Universite Grenoble Alpes","University Savoie Mont Blanc"],"keyword":["Partial Derivative Method","constant depth circuits","set multilinear polynomials","superpolynomial lower bounds","tree bias"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on the partial derivative method applied to lopsided set multilinear polynomials","src":["au","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.CCC.2022.32","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-082.65-016078","identifier":[{"id":"85134378695","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-2fc57434-c8cf-4e44-a066-5eef233fcef8","itu-64e74a5f-94af-4e9a-b966-90aaa355e182"],"keyword":["Barriers to Lower Bounds","Partial Derivative Method","constant-depth circuits","set-multilinear polynomials","superpolynomial lower bounds","tree bias"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-58D49CA9281C4B8022B8089DADCF396E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.CCC.2022.32","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"fr","key":"universite grenoble alpes","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universite Grenoble Alpes","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"fr","key":"university savoie mont blanc","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University Savoie Mont Blanc","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nutan","key":"limayepau","last":"Limaye","locid":"3f05e4ce-6433-4c5a-b72c-aa89422cf431","name":"Limaye, Nutan","orcid":"0000-0002-0238-1674","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Srikanth","key":"srinivasanpau","last":"Srinivasan","locid":"dfdcb974-8de7-4eaa-8422-1bd9daecf2f0","name":"Srinivasan, Srikanth","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Sébastien","key":"tavenaspau","last":"Tavenas","name":"Tavenas, Sébastien","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Shachar","key":"lovettpedh","last":"Lovett","name":"Lovett, Shachar","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4230/lipics.ccc.2022.32","eisbn":["9783959772419"],"issn":["18688969"],"publisher":"Dagstuhl Publishing","series":"Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics, LIPIcs","status":"p","title":"37th Computational Complexity Conference, CCC 2022"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85134378695"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-2fc57434-c8cf-4e44-a066-5eef233fcef8","itu-64e74a5f-94af-4e9a-b966-90aaa355e182"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"On the Partial Derivative Method Applied to Lopsided Set-Multilinear Polynomials","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-082.65-016078","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"An Algebraic Circuit for a polynomial P in F}[x1,.., xn] is a computational model for constructing the polynomial p using only additions and multiplications. It is a syntactic model of computation, as opposed to the Boolean Circuit model, and hence lower bounds for this model are widely expected to be easier to prove than lower bounds for Boolean circuits. Despite this, we do not have superpolynomial lower bounds against general algebraic circuits of depth 3 (except over constant-sized finite fields) and depth 4 (over fields other than F}2), while constant-depth Boolean circuit lower bounds have been known since the early 1980s. In this paper, we prove the first super polynomial lower bounds against general algebraic circuits of all constant depths over all fields of characteristic 0 (or large). We also prove the first lower bounds against homogeneous algebraic circuits of constant depth over any field. Our approach is surprisingly simple. We first prove superpolynomial lower bounds for constant-depth Set-Multilinear circuits. While strong lower bounds were already known against such circuits, most previous lower bounds were of the form f(d) poly}(N), where d denotes the degree of the polynomial. In analogy with Parameterized complexity, we call this an FPT lower bound. We extend a well-known technique of Nisan and Wigderson (FOCS 1995) to prove non-FPT lower bounds against constant-depth set-multilinear circuits computing the Iterated Matrix Multiplication polynomial IMM}n, d (which computes a fixed entry of the product of d ntimes n matrices). More precisely, we prove that any set-multilinear circuit of depth Δ computing IMM}n, d must have size at least n d\\exp(-O(Δ)}. This result holds over any field, as long as d=o(log n). We then show how to convert any constant-depth algebraic circuit of size s to a constant-depth set-multilinear circuit with a blow-up in size that is exponential in d but only polynomial in s over fields of characteristic 0. (For depths greater than 3, previous results of this form increased the depth of the resulting circuit to Ω(log s). This implies our constant-depth circuit lower bounds. Finally, we observe that our superpolynomial lower bound for constant-depth circuits implies the first deterministic sub-exponential time algorithm for solving the Polynomial Identity Testing (PIT) problem for all small depth circuits using the known connection between algebraic hardness and randomness.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Limaye, Nutan","Srinivasan, Srikanth","Tavenas, Sebastien"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-198335"],"event":{"end":"2022-02-10","place":"Virtual, Online, us","start":"2022-02-07","title":"62nd IEEE Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science, FOCS 2021","year":"2022"},"filters":{"added":"2022-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["France"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Universite Grenoble Alpes"],"keyword":["Algebraic complexity theory","Computational complexity","Lower bounds","Small depth circuits"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"superpolynomial lower bounds against low depth algebraic circuits","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85127200509","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-198335","identifier":[{"id":"85127200509","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-78a32f88-1a31-44fb-9fcf-75cd5b88cd1b"],"keyword":["Algebraic complexity theory","Computational complexity","Lower bounds","Small depth circuits"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-F075ED81A58A38BA0F897460BCEB1328"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"fr","key":"universite grenoble alpes","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universite Grenoble Alpes","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nutan","key":"limayepau","last":"Limaye","name":"Limaye, Nutan","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Srikanth","key":"srinivasanpau","last":"Srinivasan","locid":"dfdcb974-8de7-4eaa-8422-1bd9daecf2f0","name":"Srinivasan, Srikanth","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastien","key":"tavenaspau","last":"Tavenas","name":"Tavenas, Sebastien","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/focs52979.2021.00083","eisbn":["9781665420556"],"pages":"804-814","publisher":"IEEE Computer Society","status":"p","title":"Proceedings - 2021 IEEE 62nd Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science, FOCS 2021"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85127200509"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-78a32f88-1a31-44fb-9fcf-75cd5b88cd1b"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Superpolynomial Lower Bounds Against Low-Depth Algebraic Circuits","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-198335","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"An Algebraic Formula for a polynomial P [x1 xN] is an algebraic expression for P(x1 xN) using variables, field constants, additions and multiplications. Such formulas capture an algebraic analog of the Boolean complexity class NC1. Proving lower bounds against this model is thus an important problem. It is known that, to prove superpolynomial lower bounds against algebraic formulas, it suffices to prove good enough lower bounds against restricted kinds of formulas known as Set-Multilinear formulas, for computing a polynomial P(x1 xN) of degree O(logN/loglogN). In the past, many superpolynomial lower bounds were found, but they are of the form ω(f(d) poly(N)) (where f is typically a subexponential function) which is insufficient to get lower bounds for general formulas. Recently, the authors proved the first non-FPT lower bounds, i.e., a lower bound of the form Nω(f(d)), against small-depth set-multilinear formulas (and also for circuits). In this work, we extend this result in two directions. Large-depth set-multilinear formulas. In the setting of general set-multilinear formulas, we prove a lower bound of (logn)ω(logd) for computing the Iterated Matrix Multiplication polynomial IMMn,d. In particular, this implies the first superpolynomial lower bound against unbounded-depth set-multilinear formulas computing IMMn,n. As a corollary, this resolves the homogeneous version of a question of Nisan (asked in 1991) regarding the relative power of Algebraic formulas and Branching programs in the non-commutative setting. Stronger bounds for homogeneous non-commutative small-depth circuits. In the small-depth homogeneous non-commutative case, we prove a lower bound of nd1/\"/2O(\"), which yields non-FPT bounds for depths up to o(logd). In comparison, our previous bound works in the harder commutative set-multilinear setting, but only up to depths o(loglogd). Moreover, our lower bound holds for all values of d, as opposed to the previous set-multilinear lower bound, which holds as long as d is small, i.e., d = O(logn).","author_count":5,"contributors":["Tavenas, Sébastien","Limaye, Nutan","Srinivasan, Srikanth"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-204074"],"event":{"end":"2022-06-24","place":"Rome, it","start":"2022-06-20","title":"54th Annual ACM SIGACT Symposium on Theory of Computing, STOC 2022","year":"2022"},"filters":{"added":"2022-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["France"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Universite Grenoble Alpes","University of Savoy Mont Blanc"],"keyword":["Algebraic complexity"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"set multilinear and non commutative formula lower bounds for iterated matrix multiplication","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://hal.science/hal-03761732/file/paperSMNFLBIMM.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-204074","identifier":[{"id":"85132770790","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-82e50309-2862-4fb0-b52c-42e67898f446"],"keyword":["Algebraic complexity","Iterated Matrix Multiplication","Non-commutative formulas","Set-multilinear formulas"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-5-4CE4304B7377D2FB5E5BF6C77C25D83B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://hal.science/hal-03761732/file/paperSMNFLBIMM.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"fr","key":"universite grenoble alpes","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universite Grenoble Alpes","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"fr","key":"university of savoy mont blanc","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Savoy Mont Blanc","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sébastien","key":"tavenaspau","last":"Tavenas","name":"Tavenas, Sébastien","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Nutan","key":"limayepau","last":"Limaye","name":"Limaye, Nutan","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Srikanth","key":"srinivasanpau","last":"Srinivasan","locid":"dfdcb974-8de7-4eaa-8422-1bd9daecf2f0","name":"Srinivasan, Srikanth","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Stefano","key":"leonardipedh","last":"Leonardi","name":"Leonardi, Stefano","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Anupam","key":"guptapedh","last":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Anupam","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3519935.3520044","eisbn":["9781450392648"],"issn":["07378017"],"pages":"416-425","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","series":"Proceedings of the Annual ACM Symposium on Theory of Computing","status":"p","title":"STOC 2022 - Proceedings of the 54th Annual ACM SIGACT Symposium on Theory of Computing"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85132770790"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-82e50309-2862-4fb0-b52c-42e67898f446"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Set-multilinear and non-commutative formula lower bounds for iterated matrix multiplication","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-204074","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
